<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · 1873 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Home on the Range</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from 1870s Kansas about a home on the open prairie, now the state song of Kansas.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Brewster M. Higley</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Daniel E. Kelley</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>settlers, ranchers and cowboys of the American West</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>near West Beaver Creek, Smith County, Kansas, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written 1872 or 1873 (perhaps as early as 1871); published 1873 or 1874</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Brewster M. Higley, published 1873 or 1874 as &quot;My Western Home&quot;; music by Daniel E. Kelley (d. 1905))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In 1871 Dr. Brewster M. Higley moved from Indiana to Smith County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, where he took land under the Homestead Act and lived in a small cabin near West Beaver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>. Moved by the country around him, he wrote the poem &quot;My Western Home&quot;, in 1872 or 1873 by most accounts, though at least one source dates it as early as 1871. It was printed in the Smith County Pioneer in 1873 or 1874 and again in The Kirwin Chief on 21 March 1874. Higley's friend Daniel E. Kelley (1843–1905), a member of the Harlan Brothers Orchestra, worked out a melody for it on his guitar. Higley's words are close to the version sung today but not the same: the original poem did not contain the words &quot;on the range&quot;. His cabin is now on the National Register of Historic Places as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a> Cabin.</p><p>Ranchers, cowboys and other western settlers took the song up as a rural anthem, and it spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in many forms and under several names, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a>&quot; and &quot;Western Home&quot; the most common. Cowboys driving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> on the Chisholm Trail seem to have carried it from state to state. The folklorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lomax">John Lomax</a> printed it in 1910 in Cowboy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> and Other Frontier Ballads, and reported that he had learned it from a Black saloon keeper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> who remembered it from the Chisholm Trail. In 1925 the Texas composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> W. Guion arranged it as sheet music, published by G. Schirmer.</p><p>The words had been borrowed almost from the start. On 26 February 1876 The Kirwin Chief accused The Stockton News of &quot;PLAGIARISM&quot; for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> a nearly identical poem under the name of a Mrs. Emma Race of Raceburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, and reprinted the poem it had published in 1874. When Bing Crosby's recording with Lennie Hayton and his orchestra on 27 September 1933 made the song widely popular, its origins were obscure, and in 1934 William and Mary Goodwin sued for $500,000, claiming it copied their &quot;An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> Home&quot; of 1905. Investigating on behalf of the Music Publishers Protection Association, Samuel Moanfeldt also found a similar &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Home&quot;, but within a few months concluded that Higley had written the poem and Kelley had set it to music.</p><p>On 30 June 1947 &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a>&quot; became the state song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, and it is often called the unofficial anthem of the American West. Frank Sinatra, Gene Autry, Burl Ives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> and many others recorded it; it was the theme of the 1950s television series The Range Rider and is sung in films from The Awful Truth (1937) to Where the Buffalo Roam (1980, by Neil Young) and The Messenger (2009, by Willie Nelson). The University of Kansas band, the Marching Jayhawks, plays it at the end of every home athletic event. In 2010 members of the Western Writers of America chose it as one of the Top 100 western songs of all time, and a 2017 docudrama told the story of its origins and of the lawsuit.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A settler's song of contentment with life on the open plains: a home among the buffalo, deer and antelope, under clear skies and pure air, which the singer would not trade for any city. In the 1910 text one verse says, without regret, that the Native people had been driven from this part of the West and would likely never return. The mood is calm and wistful, a picture of the frontier as its settlers wished to see it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, give me a home where the buffalo roam,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the deer and the antelope play,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where seldom is heard a discouraging word</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the skies are not cloudy all day.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Home, home on the range,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the deer and the antelope play;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where seldom is heard a discouraging word</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the skies are not cloudy all day.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the air is so pure, the zephyrs so free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The breezes so balmy and light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That I would not exchange my home on the range</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For all of the cities so bright.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The red man was pressed from this part of the West,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He's likely no more to return</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the banks of Red River where seldom if ever</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their flickering camp-fires burn.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>How often at night when the heavens are bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With the light from the glittering stars,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Have I stood here amazed and asked as I gazed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If their glory exceeds that of ours.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, I love these wild flowers in this dear land of ours,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The curlew I love to hear scream,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And I love the white rocks and the antelope flocks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That graze on the mountain-tops green.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, give me a land where the bright diamond sand</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Flows leisurely down the stream;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the graceful white swan goes gliding along</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Like a maid in a heavenly dream.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then I would not exchange my home on the range,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the deer and the antelope play;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where seldom is heard a discouraging word</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the skies are not cloudy all day.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Home, home on the range,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the deer and the antelope play;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where seldom is heard a discouraging word</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the skies are not cloudy all day.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: John A. Lomax, Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads (1910), &quot;A Home on the Range&quot;. Text from <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/21300" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
